# Origin:  clearcotelabs/clearcote-browser -- the seed engine, ported; the wiring, Veil's.
# Source:  patches/001-farble-seed-core.patch (the seed arithmetic), with 060/070 as the
#          demonstration of how its seeds re-point the noise surfaces.
# Commit:  clearcote-browser-main tarball, audited 2026-08-24 (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.5)
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: the mixing function is 001's, verbatim in its arithmetic (domain hash -> biased high
#          word, hashed low word, global seed folded into the top bits); the hash of the domain
#          via base::PersistentHash and the decimal-ASCII rendering for the std::hash<string>
#          per-pixel keys are 001's too. What is deliberately not taken:
#          - the PersonaProfile struct (6.9: one identity, one source, and the source is the one
#            the UI can name) -- the seed reads --fingerprint= alone, like every other surface in
#            this set;
#          - the --disable-fingerprint-noise switch -- Veil's opt-out vocabulary is
#            --disable-spoofing, checked by each surface's own gate exactly as before 966;
#          - 001's registrable-domain helper read the domain inside components/ungoogled from a
#            GURL the caller had to build; Veil's takes the host string, because every call site
#            here holds a SecurityOrigin and net's GetDomainAndRegistry has a host overload.
#
# Veil-authored `9NN` numbering: `966` has no `066` to correct; `950`-`999` are Veil-authored with
# no counterpart. The patches it re-points are `012`/`013`/`015`+`915`/`016`/`950` -- this patch
# follows them in the series and never edits them in place.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What per-origin deterministic farbling buys, and what it costs.
#
# Before 966, every noise surface in this set derives its values from `--fingerprint=<seed>`
# alone: the same profile renders the same canvas bytes on every site it visits, and those bytes
# are one constant a fingerprinter can correlate across sites. After it, the seed is mixed with
# the page's registrable domain (eTLD+1), so the SAME profile yields DIFFERENT canvas, WebGL
# readPixels, measureText and offline-audio values on different sites -- the cross-site
# correlation link is broken -- while each site sees values that are bit-identical across
# restarts, because the derivation is pure hash arithmetic over (seed, domain). That is Brave's
# farbling model, and it is the change that makes canvas noise survive a tracker embedded in two
# of the user's sites.
#
# Scope, and the rule that governs it: the farbling seed replaces ONLY the noise derivation's
# input. Every gate (fingerprint present; the surface's --disable-spoofing key absent) stays
# exactly where it was, and the seed joins the value the caller already had: each site computes
# `SeedString(DomainForHost(origin->Host()))` from the execution context it already holds --
# the 2D context, the WebGL context, the canvas element, the blob creator's context, the video
# element, the AX object, the audits agent and the offline audio destination. Surfaces that are
# NOT per-origin on purpose: the identity surfaces (UA, fonts, GPU names -- a machine is one
# machine wherever it stands), the display block, the speech catalogue, baseLatency and 984's
# mDNS hostnames -- 966 re-points the five noise patches only, per T20's scope.
#
# The opt-out story is unchanged and is the reason `--disable-spoofing` did not need a new key:
# opting canvas out still removes the canvas surfaces and WebGL readPixels, audio still removes
# the render noise -- and with no seed present, GlobalSeed() returns 0 and every gate is closed
# exactly as before. A seedless profile farbles nothing because nothing is perturbed at all.
#
# Assertion 2 (cross-realm equality) is re-scoped in the same commit as this patch, per 6.13's
# precedent for assertion-affecting changes: main frame and Worker share the fixture's origin, so
# every noise leaf still agrees between them -- but the cross-site iframe is a second origin by
# design, and under a seed its noise leaves now MUST differ from the main frame's. That
# difference is the feature working; the harness (`tools/detection-baseline/lib/assertions.mjs`)
# scores it as a farbling check instead of a divergence. The committed `default` case has no
# seed, so nothing is perturbed and all three realms remain identical there.
#
# Measured on the local build (6.23): persona case -- main and Worker identical, iframe differs
# in every farbled leaf; seed 8 -- every farbled leaf differs from seed 424242's; the unseeded
# `default` case byte-identical to the pre-966 engine.

